    "content": "Gary Woodland underwent successful brain surgery in September 2023 to remove a benign lesion that was causing severe fear and anxiety symptoms. Despite returning to the PGA Tour in January 2024, he has faced a challenging recovery, revealing a PTSD diagnosis in March 2026 stemming from the ordeal. He continues to compete while managing ongoing mental and physical health struggles, achieving an emotional win at the 2026 Houston Open. Key Aspects of the Surgery and Recovery: The Diagnosis: In May 2023, a lesion was discovered on his brain, which was pressing on the area responsible for controlling fear and anxiety. The Surgery: On Sept. 19, 2023, Woodland underwent a craniotomy to remove the tumor. Doctors were able to remove a majority of it, though some was left to prevent potential loss of sight or motor function on his left side. Recovery and Mental Health: Woodland has been candid about his difficult recovery, which included fear, anxiety, and a diagnosis of PTSD about a year after the surgery. Return to Play: He returned to competition at the Sony Open in Hawaii in January 2024. Overcoming Challenges: Despite the mental toll, he won the 2026 Houston Open, his first title since the surgery, and continues to be an advocate for mental health awareness. Woodland received the PGA Tour’s Courage Award in February 2025 for his perseverance.",
